Learning corporate Etiquette
Corporate etiquette = the unwritten rules of how to behave at work so people respect you and want to work with you. 
1. Punctuality 
Reach 5-10 mins early for office, meetings, and calls.
If you’re running late, message before the meeting starts, not after.
Respect deadlines. If you can’t make one, tell your manager early instead of going silent.
2. Communication 
Emails: Clear subject line, greeting, short body, polite closing (“Regards”, “Thanks”). 
Chat (Slack/Teams): Keep it short, professional, and reply in reasonable time.
Reply on time: Even “Got it, will get back by evening” is better than silence.
Reply All: Use it only when everyone really needs to see your message.
3. Meetings 
Come prepared and keep your notebook handy.
Mute your mic when you’re not speaking (online meetings).
Don’t interrupt people. Wait for your turn.
Keep your camera on if the team expects it, with a clean background.
Don’t use your phone during the meeting.
4. Dress code 
When in doubt, go slightly formal. Clean, ironed, and neat beats fancy.
Check what the team actually wears on day one and match that.
5. Body language and behavior 
Firm handshake, eye contact, and a genuine smile.
Sit straight, don’t slouch, and listen actively (nod, don’t zone out).
Be polite to everyone: seniors, peers, support staff, security guards. People notice this.
6. Teamwork and attitude 
Give credit when someone helps you. Never steal credit.
Don’t gossip or badmouth colleagues or the company.
Take feedback calmly. Say “Thanks, I’ll work on that” instead of arguing.
If you don’t know something, ask. Just try Google or the docs first, then ask a clear question.
7. Professional boundaries 
Keep work and personal life separate. Don’t over-share.
Don’t message seniors at odd hours unless it’s urgent.
Keep company info confidential.
Be careful on social media. Don’t post work stuff without permission.
8. Basic office manners 
Keep your desk clean.
Phone on silent.
Take calls outside the workspace, away from people working.
Don’t eat smelly food at your desk.
Say please, thank you, and sorry when needed.

